Spec: Project/Instantiation

Purpose:
  Define HOW a concrete project instance (the actual domains, modules,
  features, components, scale, and architecture this repo's project
  model ends up holding) gets produced from a raw customer prompt — the
  PROCESS, not the resulting content. This file does not hold this
  project's own domains/modules/features (bax ./domains.sdd,
  ./modules.sdd, ./project.sdd) — it holds the reasoning pipeline that
  decides what gets created there, and how much of it is needed.

  CorePrinciple:

    PROJECT IS GENERATED, NOT PREDEFINED.

  Neither this file's own instantiation pipeline nor ./project.sdd's
  five/six-file NavigationFiles set is a mandatory checklist every
  project must fill in full — they describe what CAN exist and how it
  gets decided, not what MUST exist. A trivial project may justify only
  one or two domain/module entries; a large one may justify all of them
  plus feature/component depth. The file layout in ./project.sdd stays
  fixed as a SHAPE (bax ./project.sdd -> Portability); how much of that
  shape gets populated, and with what architecture, is what this file's
  pipeline decides per project.


Owns:
  The instantiation pipeline (prompt -> project instance)
  ProjectScale classification and its role in gating architecture choice
  Skill-applicability narrowing (Relevant -> Applicable -> Required)
  Architecture-selection trade-off flow and its human-approval gate


DoesNotOwn:
  Project hierarchy shape (Project>Domain>Module>Feature>Component)
                                                (see ./project.sdd)
  Prompt intake/analysis mechanics                (see ../prompts/prompts.sdd)
  Skill catalog content                           (see ../skills/skills.sdd)
  Global engineering-architecture principles       (see ../architecture/principles.sdd)
  This project's own resolved architecture         (see ./architecture.sdd)
  Concrete domain/module list                      (see ./domains.sdd, ./modules.sdd)
  Human-decision gate mechanics                    (see ../PROJECT.sdd -> HumanDecision)


Pipeline:

  RAW PROMPT
    -> Prompt Analysis         (bax ../prompts/prompts.sdd -> Lifecycle)
    -> Skill Discovery         (bax SkillFiltering below)
    -> Architecture Analysis   (bax ArchitectureSelection below)
    -> Scale Analysis          (bax ProjectScale below)
    -> Complexity Analysis
    -> Dependency Analysis     (bax ./dependencies.sdd)
    -> Project Decision        (human approval, bax [PI4])
    -> Project Instance        (./domains.sdd, ./modules.sdd, ./project.sdd
                                 Feature/Component entries, ./architecture.sdd,
                                 ./flows.sdd get populated)

  Each stage narrows what the next stage needs to consider — later
  stages MUST NOT re-derive conclusions already settled by an earlier
  stage (bax NoDuplication, architecture/architecture.sdd -> Principles).


ProjectScale:

  Illustrative classification only — the actual scale for any given
  project is an OUTPUT of Complexity/Dependency analysis, never an input
  assumed in advance:

    XS   trivial / single-purpose application
    S    light modular application
    M    modular monolith
    L    strong modular architecture, partial independent scaling
    XL   distributed / service-oriented architecture

  Scale drives how much of ./project.sdd's hierarchy gets populated and
  which architecture patterns are even eligible for consideration — it
  is not a target to reach.


SkillFiltering:

  Project -> Relevant Skills -> Applicable Skills -> Required Skills
    -> Architecture

  (YAGNI + Context Awareness — bax ../skills/skills.sdd. Applying the
  full skill catalog to a project regardless of its scale violates this
  file's CorePrinciple just as much as prescribing a fixed file layout
  would.)


ArchitectureSelection:

  Customer Input -> Requirements -> Complexity -> Expected Growth
    -> Team/Operational Constraints -> Architecture Candidates
    -> Trade-off Analysis -> Recommended Architecture -> Human Approval

  (bax ../PROJECT.sdd -> HumanDecision, [R9]/[R11] — architecture choice
  is exactly the class of change that MUST NOT be silently decided by
  AI.)


Rules:

  [PI1]
  AI MUST classify ProjectScale (bax ProjectScale above) from
  Complexity/Dependency analysis before proposing any architecture
  pattern — scale is a conclusion, not an assumption.

  [PI2]
  AI MUST NOT apply a pattern (DDD, microservices, event sourcing,
  CQRS, hexagonal, ...) solely because AI is familiar with it — every
  pattern proposal MUST trace back to this file's Scale/Complexity/
  Dependency analysis and cite what requirement drove it.

  [PI3]
  Files and entries under ./project.sdd's hierarchy (domains.sdd,
  modules.sdd, and this file's own Feature/Component instances) MUST
  only be populated once analysis justifies them — creating them
  speculatively "in case they're needed later" violates CorePrinciple.

  [PI4]
  A recommended architecture MUST pass through ArchitectureSelection's
  Trade-off Analysis and MUST receive human approval before it becomes
  this project's resolved architecture (bax ./architecture.sdd ->
  ViolationVsDecision, ../PROJECT.sdd -> HumanDecision).

  [PI5]
  Skill selection MUST follow SkillFiltering's Relevant -> Applicable
  -> Required narrowing — skipping straight to the full catalog is
  itself a Scale-Awareness violation, not a shortcut.

  [PI6]
  This file MUST NOT redeclare the project hierarchy shape already
  owned by ./project.sdd — it owns the PROCESS that decides how much of
  that shape gets used, not the shape itself.


Navigation:
  Project hierarchy shape:        @./project.sdd
  Prompt intake:                  @../prompts/prompts.sdd
  Skill catalog:                  @../skills/skills.sdd
  Global architecture principles: @../architecture/principles.sdd
  This project's resolved arch.:  @./architecture.sdd
  Dependency graph:                @./dependencies.sdd
  Human decision gate:            @../PROJECT.sdd -> HumanDecision


State: STEP 15

Note:
  (prompt/new/37.md — source labeled this "Yeni STEP 13", a FOURTH
  reuse of a step number already claimed by prompt/new/34.md, 35.md and
  36.md's own chunks — bax ../PROJECT.sdd -> Note, ./project.sdd ->
  Note, ./flows.sdd -> Note for the first three instances of this same
  mislabeling pattern. This repo's own sequential count treats it as
  STEP 15.

  Naming/placement decision: the source's "Project Instantiation"
  content describes a PROCESS (prompt -> project instance), not project
  SHAPE — it does not belong inside ./project.sdd, which explicitly
  owns shape only (bax ./project.sdd -> Purpose, Portability) and whose
  NavigationFiles set is a different concern (WHERE things live, not
  HOW they get decided). Rather than overwriting ./project.sdd or
  folding this into it, this content was placed in a new sibling file —
  the same pattern used at STEP 13 when architecture/principles.sdd was
  added alongside architecture/architecture.sdd instead of overwriting
  it (bax architecture/architecture.sdd -> Note).

  Rule IDs: the source used a bare, unstructured description with no
  rule-ID scheme. Assigned a new prefix, `[PI]` ("Project
  Instantiation"), confirmed free against every other prefix in this
  repo ([R] PROJECT, [C] chains, [T] tasks, [D] decisions, [S] state,
  [AP] architecture principles, [P] prompts, [PM] project model,
  [F] flows) — same disambiguation pattern used when [PM] was chosen
  over a colliding bare [P] at STEP 14.

  Deliberate departures from the source, per the standing "apply, don't
  copy" rule: the source's full pipeline diagram (CUSTOMER -> RAW
  PROMPT -> ... -> QA -> DO -> VR) was not reproduced verbatim — its
  tail end (REQUIREMENTS -> FLOWS -> TASKS -> CHAINS -> BE>API>FE>MD ->
  QA -> DO -> VR) already restates chains.sdd's DefaultFlow and
  flows.sdd's Default sequence (bax NoDuplication), so only the novel
  front half (RAW PROMPT -> ... -> PROJECT INSTANCE) was kept as this
  file's Pipeline; the source's illustrative ProjectScale examples
  (a todo app vs. a multi-tenant SaaS platform) were generalized into
  the abstract XS-XL definitions above rather than copied as concrete
  scenarios, since concrete instances belong to a real project prompt,
  not to this schema file (bax [PI3] — this file itself must not
  speculatively invent project examples); explicit Owns/DoesNotOwn
  added (absent from the source), matching this repo's established
  per-file convention (bax architecture/principles.sdd -> DoesNotOwn).

  This file's closing principle from the source — "The SDD system
  defines how a project is understood; it does not define what every
  project must look like." — was placed at ../PROJECT.sdd -> Rules as
  [R12] rather than repeated here, since PROJECT.sdd is this repo's
  constitution-level file and [R1]-[R11] already state foundational
  principles of exactly this kind (bax ../PROJECT.sdd -> Note, STEP 15
  entry).)
